# Asset provenance

## Purpose

Make sure every generated file can answer four questions: who made it, from
what, with which references, and at what cost. Regeneration, auditing, and
cost accounting all depend on those answers being on disk.

## Scope

All layers. Applies to every page render and every piece of reference art
produced through any provider, including dry-run request specs, `manual`
handoffs, and `harness-native` generations. Applies also to reference
material the user supplies.

## Core Principles

- No orphan media. An image without a generation record or a registry entry
  is a defect and MUST be given one or removed from use.
- Records are written before or with the media, never rebuilt later from
  memory.
- A record states what the tool reported. Where the tool reported nothing,
  the record says so. It NEVER contains a plausible value made up to fill a
  field.
- Provenance is history. Records are append-only and are NEVER edited to make
  a lineage look cleaner.

## Generation records

Every generation, live or dry-run, produces a record that conforms to
`schemas/generation-record.schema.json` and is stored under
`generations/<PG_ID or ENTITY_ID>/`. Its ID is `GEN_<page-id>_<attempt##>` or
`GEN_<entity-id>_<attempt##>`. Each record MUST carry:

- `provider`, and `model` as the provider reported it.
- `prompt_package`: the ID of the compiled package that was sent. The package
  lists every reference image in attachment order, by versioned asset ID and
  role, so the record leads to the full request.
- `request.summary` and, when the provider computes one, `payload_hash`.
- `response.summary`, with the provider's job ID and any error text.
- `output_render` and `media_files` for what came back.
- `cost`: currency, amount, and the two flags `estimated` and `reported`.
- `seed` when the provider exposes it.
- `status` and an ISO 8601 `created_at`.

`generate-assets.js` writes a `GEN_*.request.json` beside each record: the
prompt, the ordered reference images with their roles, and the target file.
For `harness-native` and `manual` it is the handoff package. It keeps the
request as sent after the prompt package is recompiled for a repair, which
overwrites `prompts/<provider>.yaml`.

The reference versions a package names are kept for as long as the render
exists. Deleting a request file or a referenced image orphans the record.

## Unavailable metadata

Some tools return an image and nothing else. `harness-native` and `manual`
usually report neither model, nor cost, nor seed.

- An unreported model is recorded as `model: unreported`.
- An unreported cost is `cost.amount: null` with `reported: false`. An
  estimate made before the call keeps `estimated: true`.
- An unreported seed is `seed: null`.
- Each of these is also listed in `metadata_unavailable`, which takes the
  values `model`, `cost`, and `seed`.
- A zero cost that the tool actually reported is recorded as `0` with
  `reported: true`. Null and zero mean different things.

## Reference registry

- Every reference asset is registered in `references/manifest.yaml`: masters
  generated in the project, photographs, style samples, costume references,
  and documents. Each entry gives `id`, `entity`, `kind`, `path`, and
  `status`, and SHOULD give `default_role` and `sha256`.
- An unregistered image MUST NOT be attached to a request. Register it first.
- User-supplied material arrives in `references/inbox/` and is registered by
  `/ingest-references`. The entry's `notes` record where the file came from
  and which decision it controls: identity, style, or costume.
- An approved entry carries `approved_by` and `approved_at`. A replaced entry
  stays in the registry with `status: superseded`.

## Lineage rules

- A repair render records its parent. The repair prompt package names it in
  `repair.of_render` and `edit_target`, and the new review names it in
  `supersedes`.
- Failed and rejected attempts keep their records and their files. Set the
  record's `status` to `failed` or `rejected`. NEVER delete a record or a
  render to hide a bad attempt.
- A master promoted from a generation keeps its generation record. Promotion
  copies the chosen candidate to the `_MASTER` name; the candidate file the
  record names stays in place.

## Validation

- `scripts/validate-workspace.js` schema-checks every record against
  `generation-record.schema.json`, which accepts `unreported` and `null` in
  the fields above and requires `cost.reported` and `cost.estimated`.
- `scripts/generate-assets.js` writes a record and a request file under
  `generations/` for every run, dry-run included.
- `scripts/validate-workspace.js` fails a record whose `metadata_unavailable`
  list contradicts its `model`, `cost`, or `seed` fields, and a cost amount
  that was neither reported nor marked as an estimate.
- `scripts/build-reference-plan.js` and `scripts/compile-prompts.js` look
  assets up in `references/manifest.yaml` and attach only approved ones.
  `scripts/validate-workspace.js` reports a page, reference plan, page
  prompt package, or bible file that cites an unregistered asset. In the
  registry it fails a duplicate asset ID, an `entity` that is not the prefix
  of the ID, and an approved asset whose `path` is not on disk (a warning
  for the other statuses).
- No script can tell a reported value from an invented one. `production-qa`
  compares records with what the tool returned, and unexplained gaps in a
  lineage go to human review.
